1. d4 { Edit: Thank you all for the wonderful support I'm getting! I never thought this study would reach 200 loves!! (Can we reach 300?! :o) You're all wonderful! Happy checkmating! :)
Welcome to FunnyAnimatorJimTV's Opening Systems Study. Feel free to SHARE this study with your friends.
Please click the heart button below if you enjoyed this study so that more people will know about it. Also you can follow to keep updated with the studies I create. I've just created a brand new study on a GREAT defense to play as black against white's e4 which can be found here: https://lichess.org/study/sEo8o4Rm I'd really appreciate it if you checked it out! :)
Anyway, what is an opening System? A system is a sequence of moves played in the opening where the moves made are not intuitive; they are on-the-card moves that you will most-of-the-time play no matter what moves your opponent makes. Another benefit to learning opening systems is that they require very little time to learn and start using. You can take 15 minutes to learn the theory and start playing these in tournaments. I have made chapters for the most popular opening systems and there will be moves that you make on your own. Continue to the next chapter. } *


[Event "🏆 Opening Systems YOU can USE in your games: London System"]
[Date "2017.08.28"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "D02"]
[Opening "Queen's Pawn Game: London System"]
[StudyName "🏆 Opening Systems YOU can USE in your games"]
[ChapterName "London System"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/oBsew7N6/fg6uuw76"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/FunnyAnimatorJimTV"]
[UTCDate "2017.08.28"]
[UTCTime "00:08:46"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ Welcome to the London System chapter. The cool thing about systems is that you can generally play them no matter how your opponent responds. There will be exceptions, of course, but the general moves are made systematically and you have the option of making defense moves when necessary. To start, bring a pawn to the center and open up a line for your dark-squared bishop. }
1. d4 { Correct! d4 is a very strong opening move, although not as common as e4, the king's pawn. d4 often leads to very dynamic positions. Black's most common options are shown here. } { [%cal Bd7d5,Bg8f6,Be7e6] } 1... d5 { Black can play several different moves and for most of them we are going to be making the same general moves. Now- how can you develop your dark-squared bishop? } 2. Bf4 { This is the signature move of the London System. White brings his dark-squared bishop (his bad bishop) outside his future pawn chain. This bishop often becomes an extremely powerful piece. } { [%csl Gd4,Ge3,Gf2,Yf4][%cal Ge2e3,Yf4c7] } (2. e3 { This move would block in our dark-squared bishop, which we don't want to do. } { [%csl Rc1,Re3][%cal Rc1e3] }) (2. Nf3 { That's a good move and we are, in fact, going to be playing it shortly! But let's develop the bishop first. }) (2. c4 { This is a good move, which leads to the Queen's Gambit. This is not the opening we're looking for, however. } { [%cal Bd5c4,Be7e6,Bc7c6,Bb8c6] }) (2. Nc3 { That's a logical move, developing the knight towards the center, however we want to develop our dark-squared bishop first. }) 2... Nf6 { What's the most logical developing move at this point? } 3. Nf3 { Great! White develops his knight, controls the important e5 square and gets a step closer to casting. } { [%csl Gf3,Gf4] } (3. e3 { That's a good move, and we are going to be playing it shortly! But let's develop one more piece first. }) (3. Nc3 { This move is good... But the d5 pawn is already firmly defended, and developing the queen's knight on c3 isn't going to give us any major benefits. }) 3... e6 { Black is strengthening his center and getting ready to develop his bishop. How can we strengthen our center and open up a line for another piece? } 4. e3 { Very good! We can play e3 because our dark-squared bishop is placed OUTSIDE of the pawn chain, and he is very ACTIVE. This is something not often seen in typical queen's pawn openings, where the dark-squared bishop is often stuck on either c1 or d2. This also opens up our light-squared bishop to get developed. } { [%csl Bd4,Be3,Bf2,Yf4][%cal Gf1d3,Yf4c7] } (4. e4 { A bit too ambitious. This loses a pawn after dxe4. } { [%csl Re4][%cal Rd5e4] }) (4. Nc3 { This move is acceptable, but it allows black to pin our knight with Bb4 and the knight is looking very silly. } { [%csl Rc3][%cal Gf8b4,Rb4c3] }) 4... Bb4+ { How to block the check? } { [%cal Rb4e1] } 5. c3 { Good! The bishop must retreat, and we have a nice pawn formation with our dark-squared bishop working well. } { [%csl Gf4,Bb2,Bc3,Bd4,Be3,Bf2,Rb4][%cal Gf4c7,Rb4e7] } (5. Nc3 { Not quite. Black's b4 bishop is very active pinning our knight and black can jump his knight into e4 attacking. } { [%csl Bb4,Rc3][%cal Rf6e4,Re4c3,Rb4c3,Rb4e1] }) (5. Nbd2 { This is okay, but the bishop is still in our camp. } { [%csl Bb4] }) (5. Qd2 { You must be joking. xD } { [%csl Rd2][%cal Rb4d2] }) 5... Be7 { Let's continue with our development. } 6. Bd3 { Great! We're continuing our development, have very active pieces and are ready to castle at this point. } { [%cal Gd3h7,Gd3a6,Gf4c7,Gf4h6,Bf3e5,Bf3g5,Ye1g1,Bf3d4,Bf3h4] } (6. Nbd2 { That's a good move, but let's develop our other bishop first. }) (6. Be2 { Right piece, but not the best place for it. }) (6. Bb5+ { This is met by c6, where you've wasted a move. } { [%cal Rc7c6] }) 6... O-O { How to improve our position? } 7. O-O { Very good! Our king is pretty safe now and our rook is a little more active than it was on h1. } { [%csl Gg1] } (7. Nbd2 { That's a good move, and we are going to be playing it shortly. Let's solidify our position first. }) 7... c5 { Black is putting pressure on our center, but our center is pretty solid. How to improve our position even more? } { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rc5d4] } 8. Nbd2 { Very good! Now all of our minor pieces are developed well. There's ALWAYS control of the center. } { [%csl Bd2,Bd3,Bf3,Bf4,Bg1] } (8. Re1 { That's a good developing move, but minor pieces before major ones. } { [%csl Ge1] }) 8... cxd4 { Black captures our pawn. How to recapture? } { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rc3d4,Re3d4,Rf3d4] } 9. exd4 { Nice! We have the open e-file to activate our major pieces onto. It's true; we're trading a central pawn for a flank pawn, but the central control of our pieces is equally, if not more important than the central control of our pawns. } { [%cal Gf1e1,Gd1e2,Ye1e6] } (9. cxd4 { We keep two pawns in the center this way and we open up the c-file, but there is something better. } { [%csl Gd4,Ge3,Rd5,Re6] }) (9. Nxd4 { Not quite. }) 9... Nc6 10. Re1 { Correct! We bring our rook to the central file that WE opened on the previous move. Notice how much more active our rook is here than it was on f1. } { [%cal Be1e6,Rf1f2] } (10. Qe2 { We do want to put the queen here, but the rooks are equally important. Don't forget about the rooks. }) 10... Bd7 { Black continues with his development. Let's develop more. } 11. Qe2 { Correct! Our pieces are active. } (11. Qc2 { This move is good and pins black's f6 knight to the defense of his h7 pawn, but we need more centralization. } { [%csl Rc2,Rd3,Rh7,Bf6][%cal Rc2h7,Gf6h7] }) 11... Rc8 { Black is developing also. At any time of all of this, black can play Nh5 and try to win our dark-squared bishop. In the opening, bishops are ever-so-slightly stronger than the knights, and our bishop is extremely strong on the h2-b8 diagonal, so we don't want him to take our bishop. How can we save it in case he plays Nh5? } { [%csl Rf4][%cal Rf6h5,Yh5f4] } 12. h3 { Yes! Now the bishop can hide at h2 if he is attacked. } (12. h4 { Right idea, but the h pawn is pretty weak here and the king is being exposed more. }) (12. Be3 { The bishop was the most active on the h2-b8 diagonal. Why move it from there? Best to allow it to stay there safely. }) 12... Nh5 { Now the point of playing h3 is revealed. } { [%csl Gh2][%cal Bf4h2] } 13. Bh2 { And this rounds up the essentials of the London System. White has a solid position, active pieces and Stockfish gives white a 0.7 advantage. From here white can go for a kingside attack with Ne5 followed by f4, or something else depending on the position. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Gf3e5,Yf2f4,Gd4e5,Gd2f3] } *


[Event "🏆 Opening Systems YOU can USE in your games: Hippopotamus Defense"]
[Date "2017.08.29"]
