[Opening "Owen Defense"]
[StudyName "🏆 Opening Systems YOU can USE in your games"]
[ChapterName "Hippopotamus Defense"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/oBsew7N6/mL5Z211f"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/FunnyAnimatorJimTV"]
[UTCDate "2017.08.29"]
[UTCTime "15:49:31"]

1. e4 { The Hippopotamus Defense ("Hippo") has become an increasingly popular opening nowadays. Conceived in the 1960s, its extremely passive, hippo-like nature led many to believe it was ridiculous. However, times have changed. More ideas have come forward, and the opening contains lots of customization. You can tinker with this opening to suit yourself and the position. The cool thing about it is that you can play it as either white or black! :D The hippo player must have good timing, patience and a good sense of humor to play this opening. } 1... b6 { What is this move?? Black is obviously going to fianchetto his bishop to b7, but white is going to build up a strong center in the process... } { [%csl Re4,Rd4][%cal Yc8b7,Rd2d4,Rb1c3,Rg1f3] } 2. Nc3 Bb7 3. d4 e6 { [%csl Rd5][%cal Re6d5] } 4. Bd3 g6 { [%csl Gb7,Gg7][%cal Gf8g7] } 5. Nf3 Bg7 6. Be3 Ne7 { [%csl Ge7][%cal Ge8g8] } 7. O-O O-O 8. Re1 h6 { [%csl Rg5][%cal Rh6g5] } 9. Qe2 d6 { [%csl Be6,Bd6,Yf5,Ye5,Yd5,Yc5] } 10. Rad1 Nd7 { [%csl Ge7,Gd7] } 11. a3 { [%cal Ga7a6] } 11... a6 { This is the typical Hippo setup. We move a number of pawns to our 6th rank, a number of pieces to our 7th rank, being very passive and maneuvering, like a hippo, but we're waiting for the right time to strike! } { [%csl Bg7,Gh6,Gg6,Ge6,Gd6,Gb6,Ga6,Bb7,Bd7,Be7] } 12. d5 { White has pulled the trigger! } { [%csl Ge4,Gd5,Re6][%cal Rd5e6] } (12. e5 { White is pushing forward! } { [%csl Ge5,Rd6][%cal Re5d6,Ge5f6] } 12... d5 { Black has closed the position and started his plan to play on the queenside with a c5 thrust and to activate his knights. } { [%csl Ge6,Gd5,Be5,Bd4,Rc5][%cal Ge7c6,Gd7c5,Rc7c5,Ba8c8,Rb6b5,Ra6a5,Rb5b4] }) 12... e5 { Locked! As you can see, black has closed up the center. White can't do anything in the center, and this gives black the time to strike on the wing with an f5 thrust. Similarly, if white had played e5 instead of d5 (sideline ->) black would close the center with d5 and play for a c5 thrust on the queenside. The PAWNS POINT to the side of the board you want to play on. This rounds up the Hippopotamus Defense chapter. } { [%csl Gd6,Ge5,Bd5,Be4,Rf5][%cal Gd7f6,Ge7f5,Rf7f5] } *


