[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/oBsew7N6/5GzkqIEB"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/FunnyAnimatorJimTV"]
[UTCDate "2017.08.28"]
[UTCTime "00:08:56"]

1. d4 { Welcome to the Lion System chapter. The Lion System, also known as the "Black Lion" is an opening for black that can be used against white's move d4, and in certain cases e4. The move setup is easy to perform and is going to be quite unfamiliar to white. } 1... c6 { This move looks strange and passive, but it gives black a compact center soon. } 2. e4 { This is probably going to be white's most natural-looking response (along with c4) as black is basically handing white the center here... } 2... d6 { Black is restraining white's pawns and freeing squares for his pieces to sit on his 7th rank. } { [%csl Bd7,Bc7,Gb5,Gc5,Gd5,Ge5] } 3. Nc3 { Once again, the moves that white plays aren't going to differ the position too much... } 3... Qc7 { It may seem immature that we're developing the queen before all else, but the queen is not endangering herself and she supports the area very well. } { [%csl Gc7][%cal Gd8c7] } 4. Nf3 { [%cal Rc8g4,Gg1f3] } 4... Bg4 { Whenever white plays his knight to f3 in the Lion, you want to pin the knight with Bg4. } { [%csl Rf3][%cal Bg4d1] } 5. h3 { [%csl Rg4][%cal Rh3g4] } 5... Bh5 { [%cal Gh5d1] } 6. Bc4 { Now we want to develop our pieces. } { [%cal Gb8d7] } 6... Nd7 { Now we have more pieces developed. We want to follow Nd7 with an e5 thrust. } { [%cal Re7e5] } 7. Be3 { [%cal Be7e5] } 7... e5 { Nd7 and e5 moves go together. In some circumstances you can alternate these move orders. } { [%csl Bd7,Be5][%cal Bb8d7,Be7e5] } 8. g4 { [%csl Rh5][%cal Rg4h5] } 8... Bg6 { [%csl Ye4][%cal Yg6e4] } 9. Qd2 { We need more development. } { [%csl Bg8][%cal Gg8f6] } 9... Ngf6 { Yes! We develop more of our army and at the same time attack white's e4 pawn in this case. White must move a piece a second time. } { [%csl Re4][%cal Rg6e4,Rf6e4,Gc3e4] } 10. Bd3 { Now we must develop our last minor piece. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Bf8e7] } 10... Be7 { Excellent! This concludes my Lion System chapter. Now black can castle in either direction depending on the position. For example, if white has castled on the kingside, you can go for a safe game by castling in the same direction (kingside) and go for the queenside push of your a and b pawns. If white has castled on the queenside, you can play it safe and go for the same side castle, and plan on for the central break with d5. Either way, you can play for an aggressive game and go for the opposite-side-castle and respective plans. } { [%cal Ge8g8,Be8c8,Ge1g1,Be1c1,Gb7b5,Ga7a5,Ga5a4,Bd6d5] } *


[Event "🏆 Opening Systems YOU can USE in your games: King's Indian Attack"]
