[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ Welcome to FunnyAnimatorJimTV's Opening Systems Study. What is an opening System? A system is a sequence of moves played in the opening where the moves made are not intuitive; they are on-the-card moves that you will most-of-the-time play no matter what moves your opponent makes. Another benefit to learning opening systems is that they require very little time to learn and start using. You can take 15 minutes to learn the theory and start playing these in tournaments. I have made chapters for the most popular opening systems and there will be moves that you make on your own. Continue to the next chapter. }
1. e4 { Black has a variety of responses to this, but against all of them our plan of campaign will be nearly identical. } { [%cal Ge2e4,Be7e5,Be7e6,Bc7c5,Bc7c6,Bd7d6,Bd7d5] } 1... e5 { Black's most common response. Now, how can we STRENGTHEN OUR CENTER? } 2. d3 { Yes! It may look like we're rendering our bishop inactive, but we're going to use this bishop as a king defender. Our center is strong. } { [%csl Gc2,Gd3,Ge4,Rf1][%cal Rf1d3,Yg2g3,Yf1g2] } (2. Nf3 { That's a great move, and the most common! However we're looking for something a bit different. }) (2. Nc3 { Good, we're overprotecting our e4 pawn. This would be the Vienna Game. However, we're looking for something a bit different. }) (2. f4 { King's Gambit! A very aggressive opening, but not what we're looking for. }) (2. g3 { This is one of the ideas of the "King's Indian", fianchettoing our bishop. But we need to strengthen our center first. }) (2. c3 { A cool looking move, but not what we're looking for. }) 2... Nf6 { Now we need to develop our pieces. } 3. Nf3 { Excellent! Piece development is more important than pushing pawns. } { [%cal Gg1f3] } (3. g3 { We are going to be playing this move shortly, but let's develop actively first. :) }) (3. Nc3 { You have the right idea, but there's a more important piece that needs to be developed. }) (3. Be3 { That's a developing move, but knights before bishops. }) (3. Nd2 { In fact, we are going to be playing this move shortly! Let's develop the king's knight first. }) 3... Nc6 { Let's continue with development. } 4. Nbd2 { Correct! The King's Indian Attack is a more passive than aggressive opening, so white doesn't often place his pieces on their "most active" squares. } (4. Nc3 { That's a more active square for the knight, but it's not in the King's Indian Attack theory. The King's Indian Attack is more passive than active, despite the name. :P }) (4. g3 { We are going to be playing that move shortly, but knights before bishops. }) 4... Bc5 { Black is developing well. Time to continue with our development! } 5. g3 { Yes! Our bishop will be a good king guardian on g2 after we've castled, similar to in the King's Indian Defense. } { [%cal Gg2g3,Bf1g2,Be1g1] } (5. Be2 { That's developing the bishop, but it doesn't fit with our "King's Indian" theme. }) 5... d6 { Next move should be pretty obvious :) } 6. Bg2 { Exactly! Now our bishop is fulfilling king defense. REMEMBER; white's first few moves are built upon the objective of creating an extremely safe defense around his king, and ONLY THEN proceeding to create an attack on a wing. } { [%csl Bg2][%cal Ge1g1] } (6. Be2 { We played g3 for a reason. }) 6... Be6 { Now what? :) } 7. O-O { Wonderful! In "King's Indian" setups, the player's king is often extremely safe, with a very good guardian on g2. } { [%csl Gg2,Yg1] } 7... Qd7 { Now the King's Indian Attack setup is nearly completed. We need to solidify our center... } 8. c3 { Nice! Our structure here is pretty solid and white's position has little risk. His king is safe, his pieces are developed and the plan is as follows: If black has castled on the kingside, white will go for a kingside attack with Nh4 followed by f4, where the f-pawn will run down the board. If black has castled on the queenside, white will go for a queenside attack by throwing the a and b pawns up the board. This concludes the King's Indian Attack chapter. } { [%cal Gf2f4,Gf4f5,Rf3h4,Bb2b4,Ba2a4,Ba4a5,Re8g8,Be8c8] } *


