[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/oBsew7N6/CIqDxLY3"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/FunnyAnimatorJimTV"]
[UTCDate "2017.08.28"]
[UTCTime "00:09:33"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { I'm sure this looks familiar to you... } 3. Nc3 Nf6 { We have reached a four knights game. It seems pretty standard, until... } { [%csl Bc3,Bf3,Bf6,Bc6] } 4. g3 { What is this?! This is a pretty strange-looking move. White clearly wants to place a bishop on g2, but it'll be looking at its own pawn... 4. g3 is known as the Glek System. It's quite interesting. } { [%csl Re4][%cal Yf1g2,Yg2e4] } 4... d5 { If black ever wants to break the center with a d5 thrust, this is all the better for us! } { [%csl Re4][%cal Rd5e4] } 5. exd5 Nxd5 { [%csl Gc3][%cal Bd5c3,Yf1g2] } 6. Bg2 { So it turns out that by exchanging on d5, black has helped white's bishop in becoming more active. Neat! } { [%cal Gg2a8] } 6... Nxc3 { [%csl Rd1][%cal Rd2c3,Gd8d1,Re1d1] } 7. bxc3 { By recapturing with the b-pawn, white has gained the open b-file to place his rook on. } { [%cal Yb1b7,Ga1b1] } 7... Bd7 8. Rb1 { This rook on the b-file is very well placed and active, and works well in conjunction with the g2 bishop. If, for example, black plays b6 to defend the pawn... } { [%csl Rb7,Gg2,Gb1][%cal Rb1b7,Gg2a8] } 8... b6 { Your bishop REALLY opens up a lot. There are no pawns in the way of it controlling the ENTIRE a8-h1 diagonal. White is very active here. } { [%cal Rg2a8] } 9. O-O { King safety. } 9... Bc5 10. Re1 { Controlling the central open file. } { [%csl Re5][%cal Re1e5] } 10... f6 11. d3 { [%csl Gc4,Ge4,Gd4,Gb4] } 11... O-O 12. Bd2 { The idea here is to develop the other bishop on the other long diagonal. The Glek gives white (and black) very dynamic possibilities. } { [%csl Be1,Bf3,Bg2,Bd2,Bb1,Rg8,Rf7,Re6][%cal Gc3c4,Gd2c3] } *


[Event "🏆 Opening Systems YOU can USE in your games: Mystery System"]
