[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B24"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Closed"]
[StudyName "🏆 Opening Systems YOU can USE in your games"]
[ChapterName "Mystery System"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/oBsew7N6/6PvvLGs7"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/FunnyAnimatorJimTV"]
[UTCDate "2017.08.28"]
[UTCTime "00:43:07"]

1. e4 { This system doesn't really have a name; it's just a system that you can occasionally use. This system could work against things like the King's Indian Attack, the English Opening, The Closed Sicilian, etc. It won't work against any opening where white has a pawn on d4 or gets an early d4 (Open Sicilian, etc.), for reasons we'll get into. } 1... c5 { We're not intentionally playing the Sicilian Defense here, but we are playing something similar to a few of its variations. Note that if white plays the Smith Morra Gambit with an early d4, this system will not work; you'll have to take the pawn and continue with intuitive play. } 2. Nc3 (2. Nf3 { Here we're seeing what happens if black uses this system against white's early d4. } 2... Nc6 { [%cal Rd2d4] } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 { [%csl Rd4][%cal Yd1d6] } 4... g6 5. Nc3 Bg7 6. Be3 e6 { It is going to cause black huge problems if he uses this system against white's playing d4. The reason is, black cannot play the move e6 without creating enormous weaknesses on the d-file, which white has open BECAUSE he exchanged his d-pawn on d4. White can jump his knight into b5-d6. The knight is incredibly strong here, causes black enormous headaches, and white can use his major pieces on the d-file to attack black's weakness on d7. This is a disgusting position for black. } { [%csl Rd6,Rb5,Rd7][%cal Rd4b5,Gb5d6,Rd1d6,Ra1d1,Rd1d2,Rd5d7] }) 2... Nc6 3. g3 g6 { [%cal Gf8g7] } 4. Bg2 Bg7 { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rg7d4,Rc5d4,Rc6d4,Yd2d4] } 5. Nge2 e6 { [%csl Rd6][%cal Re7e6,Rc7c6] } 6. O-O Nge7 { [%cal Ge8g8] } *


[Event "🏆 Opening Systems YOU can USE in your games: Gurgenidze System"]
[Date "2017.08.29"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B12"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Advance Variation"]
[StudyName "🏆 Opening Systems YOU can USE in your games"]
[ChapterName "Gurgenidze System"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/oBsew7N6/ryKdsBQJ"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/FunnyAnimatorJimTV"]
