[UTCTime "17:03:16"]

1. e4 { This is a pretty strong system. It will generally work against any opening where white has a pawn on e4 and he pushes it to e5. This could derive from the f4 Sicilian or the Caro Kann (what we'll be looking at). All of the MAIN MOVES & IDEAS that you must play in this system will be MARKED IN BLUE. } 1... c6 2. d4 d5 { Getting a pawn on d5... } { [%csl Bd5] } 3. e5 h5 { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bh5g4] } 4. Nf3 Bg4 { You want to bring this bishop out and swap it off for either white's bishop or knight. Swapping off our bad bishop. } { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bg4f3] } 5. Nbd2 e6 { [%csl Be6] } 6. h3 { [%csl Rg4][%cal Rh3g4] } 6... Bxf3 { Whenever they play h3, we take on f3. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg4f3] } 7. Nxf3 g6 { [%csl Bg6] } 8. Bd3 Nh6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bh6f5] } 9. c3 Nf5 { The idea of this entire game will revolve around black's knight on f5. White is going to do his best to dislodge it, and black is going to do his best to make it invincible. Black can try to play for Nc6-e7, supporting the f5 knight or use his, while white is going to do his best to play for a g4 push. As for black's other pieces... Black's bishop will go on e7 where it controls strong diagonals. Black's rook is very well placed on h8, so black's king won't castle; instead Ke8-f8-g7. Black's queenside will be developed with a6, controlling b5. From here black will plan for c5 and to swap off white's d4 pawn. From here black will develop his rook to c8 and queen to b6. A long with the knight on c6 and the knight on f4, black can build tremendous pressure against white's d4 pawn. } { [%csl Bf5,Be8,Yf8,Gh8,Yg4,Gc6,Rb5][%cal Bb8c6,Bc6e7,Be7f5,Be8f8,Bf8g7,Yf8e7,Rd8b6,Ra8c8,Yh5g4,Rg2g4,Gc6c5,Ga7a6,Ra6b5] } *


